Because Wi-Fi devices share transmission time, not bandwidth.


PHY Rate
radio transmission speed at Layer 1 (Physical Layer)
PHY Rate Depends On
1. Channel Width
2. Modulation Scheme (MCS)
3. Spatial Streams (MIMO)
4. Guard Interval
5. Signal Quality (RSSI/SNR)


1. Co-Channel Contention (CCC)
This is the normal behavior in Wi-Fi when multiple APs/devices use the same channel.
Example:
AP1 → Channel 36
AP2 → Channel 36
Both are using the same frequency.
Wi-Fi uses CSMA/CA:
Before transmitting, devices listen first.
If AP1 is transmitting:
AP2 waits
clients wait
So devices are sharing airtime politely.

AP1  ))))))   AP2
Both can hear each other
→ Take turns

2. Co-Channel Interference (CCI)
This happens when devices on same channel cannot coordinate properly.
Usually because:
APs are too far apart
hidden node problem
weak RSSI
overlapping coverage badly designed
Bad Case → Interference
Now AP1 and AP2 are far apart.
AP1 cannot hear AP2 properly.
So:
AP1 starts transmitting
AP2 ALSO starts transmitting
Now at client location:
both signals arrive together
frames become unreadable

This is interference.

AP1  ))))     ((((  AP2
Cannot hear properly
→ Simultaneous transmission
→ Corruption


RSSI = Received Signal Strength Indicator
It tells:
How strong the received Wi-Fi signal is.
Measured in:
dBm
Examples:
-40 dBm → very strong
-55 dBm → good
-67 dBm → okay for voice/video
-70 dBm → weak
-80 dBm → very poor

SNR = Signal to Noise Ratio
It tells:
How clean/good quality the signal is compared to noise.
Formula:
SNR=Signal Power−Noise Power
Measured in:
dB
Simple Meaning
Suppose:
Signal = -40 dBm
Noise = -90 dBm
Then:
SNR=(−40)−(−90)=50 dB
50 dB SNR is excellent.


OFDM:
First Problem:
Why Old Wireless Systems Were Inefficient
Imagine old radio transmission:

One large carrier frequency
sending all data.

Problem:
interference affects whole signal
reflections cause corruption
high-speed transmission becomes difficult
multipath destroys signal quality

Old Single-Carrier Problem
One large carrier:
high symbol rate
very sensitive to multipath
reflections corrupt entire transmission
This became major issue as speeds increased.

OFDM Solution

Instead of:
one very fast carrier

OFDM uses:
MANY small slower subcarriers
This is the key idea.

Analogy

Instead of:

one truck carrying everything

OFDM uses:
many smaller trucks in parallel
Why This Helps
Each subcarrier:
lower symbol rate
more resistant to reflections
more robust to multipath

VERY Important Understanding

OFDM does NOT mainly increase speed initially.
Its BIGGEST advantage:
robustness against multipath/interference
This is critical understanding.

Orthogonal Meaning
Very important word.
Orthogonal
means:
subcarriers do not interfere with each other
even though closely packed.

Why Important?

Without orthogonality:
subcarriers overlap destructively.
Orthogonality allows:
efficient spectrum usage
dense packing
high efficiency

Subcarriers
Each subcarrier:
carries partial data
modulated independently

Examples:
BPSK
QPSK
QAM

So:
modulation happens PER subcarrier
Important Hidden Insight
Even if few subcarriers affected by interference:
whole transmission may still survive
Huge reliability advantage.

Guard Interval (GI)

VERY important OFDM concept.
Remember multipath echoes?

OFDM adds:

Guard Interval
small waiting gap between symbols.
Purpose:
allow delayed reflections to die out
before next symbol.
Analogy
Person speaks:
HELLO ...pause...
WORLD
Pause prevents echo overlap.
Same in OFDM.